# Host-side unit tests

These tests run on the development machine, not on the Nano. They cover every
class in `src/`, need no hardware attached, and finish in well under a second.

## Running them

```
test\run_tests.bat
```

or, equivalently, build and run by hand:

```
clang++ -std=c++17 -I test/support -I src -o test/run_tests.exe ^
    test/test_main.cpp test/test_*.cpp ^
    src/Axis/Axis.cpp src/AxisDriver/AxisDriver.cpp src/Joystick/Joystick.cpp ^
    src/Link/JoystickFrame.cpp src/Link/LinkMaster.cpp src/Link/LinkSlave.cpp ^
    src/PositionStore/PositionStore.cpp ^
    -Xlinker /subsystem:console
test\run_tests.exe
```

The script exits non-zero when a test fails, so it can be dropped straight into
CI.

## How it works

`test/support/` contains stand-ins for the four Arduino headers the application
includes:

| Header | What the shim does |
|---|---|
| `Arduino.h` | virtual `millis()`, settable `digitalRead()`/`analogRead()`, recorded `digitalWrite()`, assignable `SREG` |
| `Servo.h` | records every `write()`, so a test can assert on commanded angles |
| `EEPROM.h` | a real 1 KiB array that counts changed bytes |
| `Wire.h` | a byte queue for the receive side, a captured buffer for the send side |

Nothing about the production sources changes: `-I test/support` simply means
`#include <Arduino.h>` resolves to the shim instead of the AVR framework.

`test/support/TestFramework.h` is a small assertion framework so the suite
builds with nothing but a C++17 compiler - no PlatformIO, no Unity.

## What is covered

| File | Class |
|---|---|
| `test_axis.cpp` | `Axis` - limits, clamping, one-degree stepping, no wrap-around |
| `test_axis_driver.cpp` | `AxisDriver` - dead zone, direction, speed curve, pacing |
| `test_joystick_frame.cpp` | `JoystickFrame` - wire format, round-trip, rejection of corrupt frames |
| `test_joystick.cpp` | `Joystick` - dead band, button debouncing, millis() wrap |
| `test_position_store.cpp` | `PositionStore` - save/restore, validation, debounce |
| `test_link_slave.cpp` | `LinkSlave` - receive, publish, reject, counters |
| `test_link_master.cpp` | `LinkMaster` - addressing, acknowledgement, link state |

Several tests are deliberate regressions guards:

* `Axis_Rotate.never_wraps_*` - a positional SG90 must clamp, never wrap.
* `PositionStore_Debounce.does_not_save_while_the_pose_keeps_changing` and
  `a_stress_run_does_not_storm_the_eeprom` - EEPROM writes must not happen
  during motion. Blocking writes starved the I2C path and froze the board
  under a fast stress test.

## Notes

* `LinkSlave` exposes a single `friend struct LinkSlaveTestHook` so the suite can
  invoke the private handler that the I2C interrupt would call. It costs nothing
  at runtime and is the only test hook in the codebase.
* `AxisDriver` documents one quirk by test: a freshly constructed driver refuses
  its first step until one full pacing period has elapsed, because `lastStepMs_`
  and `millis()` both start at 0. On hardware this costs at most one frame.
